+          <img src="opvr-headset.webp" width="1500" height="844" loading="lazy" alt="Concept rendering of a modular graphite VR and mixed reality headset with a translucent panel showing a proposed photonic board and heat sink">
+          <figcaption class="muted">Illustration of a future headset concept. The photonic board and enclosure are conceptual design elements.</figcaption>
+        </figure>
+        <section class="opvr-stack" aria-labelledby="opvr-stack-title">
+          <h3 id="opvr-stack-title">How the system fits together</h3>
+          <div class="opvr-layer"><strong>Open game distribution</strong><p>People and AI agents can publish, modify and share free VR games in an open catalog with signed releases, license metadata and provenance. Games execute within the platform’s sandbox.</p></div>
+          <div class="opvr-link"><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span> Install a native game or open a web game</div>
+          <div class="opvr-lanes">
+            <div class="opvr-layer"><strong>Native path</strong><p>Linux hosts the application and device drivers. The game uses OpenXR, with Monado as the runtime for tracking and compositing.</p></div>
+            <div class="opvr-layer"><strong>Browser path</strong><p>WebXR provides immersive sessions, WebGPU submits graphics or compute work, and WebAssembly runs application code. Device and browser support must be verified.</p></div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="opvr-link"><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span> Submit work through runtime and device drivers</div>
+          <div class="opvr-layer"><strong>Electronic CPU, GPU and memory</strong><p>Handle tracking and application logic, geometry, shading, compositing and the stereo display. A host driver and explicit runtime integration would be needed to make a custom accelerator available to a browser.</p>
+            <dl class="opvr-modules">
+              <div><dt>OPA</dt><dd>Candidate accelerator for AI inference tasks, such as processing assets for generated worlds.</dd></div>
+              <div><dt>OPA-M</dt><dd>Candidate photonic data link between electronic memory and accelerator endpoints.</dd></div>
+              <div><dt>OPA-W</dt><dd>Candidate photonic weight storage for AI models, subject to retention, precision and readout validation.</dd></div>
+              <div><dt>OPA-G</dt><dd>Candidate graphics accelerator for BVH ray–box tests, with electronic fallback near ambiguous boundaries.</dd></div>
+            </dl>
+          </div>
+          <div class="opvr-link"><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span> Present completed stereo frames</div>
+          <div class="opvr-layer"><strong>Headset display and optics</strong><p>Measure frame times alongside motion-to-photon latency, thermal limits, mass and battery life on sustained workloads.</p></div>
+        </section>
